 "cells": [
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "# Polyglot Programming\n\nA Crush program can embed code from other languages with `@language { ... }`\nblocks. This chapter describes what actually happens when you run one today —\nwhich languages work, how values cross the boundary, how errors and timeouts\nbehave, and, importantly, **how much isolation you do and don't get**.\n\n> **Status: alpha, and the security story is the part to read carefully.** By\n> default a polyglot block is an ordinary subprocess running with *your* user's\n> authority. An optional bubblewrap sandbox exists but is **off by default** and\n> not enabled by any command-line flag. See [Sandbox and authority](#sandbox-and-authority).\n\n## The model in one paragraph\n\nA block's source text is handed to a real interpreter — `python3 -c`, `node -e`,\nor `bash -c` — as a **child process**. Crush marshals some variables in, runs the\ninterpreter, captures its standard output, and (for Python and JavaScript)\nmarshals one result variable back. There is no embedded WebAssembly runtime, no\nWASI capability bridge, and no in-process interpreter.\n\n## Languages\n\n| Block | Runs | Aliases |\n|---|---|---|\n| `@python { }` | `python3 -c` | `@python3`, `@py` |\n| `@javascript { }` | `node -e` | `@js`, `@node`, `@es6`, `@ecmascript` |\n| `@bash { }` | `bash -c` | `@sh` |\n\nThe interpreter must be on `PATH`. Any other tag (`@rust`, `@go`, `@c`, …) fails\nat run time with `unknown capability: no executor registered for language\n'rust'`.\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "@rust {\n    fn main() {}\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:35",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@rust` blocks (polyglot), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_error": "no executor registered for language 'rust'"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "(crush-ast also ships **walkers** for Rust, Go, C, Zig, Wasm and others. A walker\ntranslates a *source file* into CAST for compilation — it is a separate\nmechanism, driven by the `crush-walker` tool, and is not what an `@lang { }`\nblock does.)\n\n## Enabling polyglot execution\n\nPolyglot is **off by default**, because running another interpreter is full\nambient authority. Turn it on per run:\n\n```sh\ncrush run --polyglot program.crush        # same flag on crush-run\n```\n\nWithout it the block is refused:\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-2",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "fn main() {\n    @python {\n    print(\"hi\")\n    }\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "expected-failure"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:58",
      "browser": "fail",
      "why": "this example shows a refusal",
      "expect_error": "requires the 'polyglot.python' capability"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-5",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "An embedding host enables it explicitly with\n`HostCapsBuilder::polyglot(&[\"python\", \"javascript\", \"bash\"])`; each language is\ngated by its own capability name (`polyglot.python`, `polyglot.javascript`,\n`polyglot.bash`).\n\n## Output\n\nWhatever the block writes to standard output appears in the program's output, in\norder. Two details to know: the block's output is **trimmed** (so it carries no\ntrailing newline), and anything written to standard error is discarded when the\nblock succeeds.\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "print(\"one\")\n@python {\nprint(\"two\")\n}\nprint(\"three\")\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:77",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@python` blocks (polyglot), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_output": "one\ntwothree\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-7",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "If you want the following output on a new line, end the block's output with an\nexplicit blank line (`print()`), or print from Crush.\n\n## Passing values in and out\n\n### Python and JavaScript\n\nFor `@python` and `@javascript` blocks, Crush analyses the block's own source:\n\n- **In:** every name the block *reads* that is a Crush variable already in scope\n  (a `let`, a parameter, a loop variable, or an earlier block's result) is\n  injected, with its type preserved.\n- **Out:** the **last name bound at the top level of the block** is marshaled\n  back and becomes a Crush variable of that name. Earlier assignments in the same\n  block do not escape.\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-4",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "let base = 5\n@python {\nimport math\ndoubled = base * 2\nresult = math.pow(base, 3)\n}\nprint(result)\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:108",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@python` blocks (polyglot); uses the standard library (`math.pow`), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_output": "125.0\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-9",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "Here `base` goes in, `result` (the last top-level binding) comes out, and\n`doubled` is local to the block — using it afterwards is a compile error\n(`Undefined variable`).\n\nValues travel as JSON, so the shareable types are exactly these:\n\n| Crush | Python | JavaScript |\n|---|---|---|\n| Int, Float | `int`, `float` | `number` |\n| String | `str` | `string` |\n| Bool | `bool` | `boolean` |\n| Null | `None` | `null` |\n| Array | `list` | `Array` |\n| Object | `dict` | `Object` |\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-5",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "let n = 5\nlet tags = [\"a\", \"b\"]\nlet cfg = {\"k\": 1}\n@python {\nout = [n * 2, len(tags), cfg[\"k\"], None, True]\n}\nprint(out)\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "needs-host"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/polyglot.md:139",
      "browser": "host",
      "why": "uses `@python` blocks (polyglot), which the in-browser runtime does not provide",
      "expect_output": "[10, 2, 1, null, true]\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-md-11",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "An output variable that can't be serialised to JSON (a class instance, an open\nfile, a function) fails the block with a clear error rather than being dropped:\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-polyglot-6",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "@python {\nclass C:\n    pass\nobj = C()\n}\nprint(1)\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
